<!--Post: 4526--><!--Time: 1106484696-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4526"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Scbenson2001<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Scbenson2001<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 23, 2005 - 4:51 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->BTK I believe would never leave Wichita for any length of time simply because of the value it has to him in his view of the world. I think BTK thinks of this as his career. He feels he is superior to the rest of humanity. This feeling is bolstered by his proximity to his old &#34;haunts&#34; as Tom calls them. He could never be away from them for any period of time. They help him level out during stressful event in his life. When he feels out of control in his everyday life or less superior he would drive by these places. Gary Ridgeway the Green River Killer described this same type of behavior after his arrest. He also never moved away for the area of his murders even after he had become a suspect. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
